    "created_at": "2023-09-09T14:02:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-09-09T14:03:08Z",
    "body": "Um, given that I'm writing a web-extension which runs on all webpages, the main concerns of mine are:\r\n1. I'm not familiar with the current effects of `[popover=manual]', now that it will introduce a new \"top-level layer\"\r\n- 1. I also worry about its detailed behavior on Chrome 109-113, on which `[popover]` is still an experimental feature\r\n2. Maybe `[popover]` will introduce more features in the future\r\n- 1. then a current `<div opover=manual style=\"xxx\">` may not work \"exactly as I expect now\" on a future browser\r\n- 2. maybe a future website writes some rules (HTTP headers or `<link>` elements) and then break my calling on `showPopover`\r\n3. Some 3rd-party browsers may inject their own \"default style sheets\" and then affect `[popover]` elements\r\n- 1. for example, I once noticed that `Waterfox` injects some root-level CSS variables through its `userChrome.css`, breaking some UI elements of my extension\r\n4. On the contrary, the effect `position:fixed; z-index: 2147483647` is much more stable and then reliable\r\n\r\nAnyway, I think the first solution of ```adding `composed: true` to \"toggle\" event``` won't do any harm, so why not?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
